**New Evidence**
- 2022-10-16 program articulation: Xi’s report set the next five years around high-quality development, tech self-reliance, better public services, common prosperity, security, and greater international influence.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CPC expounds on main objectives, tasks for next 5 years","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-10-16","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/topnews/202210/16/content_WS634ba3e5c6d0a757729e14cc.html","claim":"The CPC’s report to the 20th National Congress set out the main objectives and tasks for the next five years, including high-quality economic development, greater self-reliance in science and technology, more equitable public services, a better social security system, and stronger international influence.","locator":"lines 45-60","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official publication with specific dated objectives and direct program language, though still self-authored regime material.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and explicitly framed as the next five-year program.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution: the report was presented by Xi Jinping on behalf of the CPC central leadership.","contrary_evidence":["The document states goals, not execution, so it does not by itself prove implementation success."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.10"]}
- 2022-10-23 closing-session resolution: the congress said the report was a political declaration and program of action, and reaffirmed Xi core leadership and the party’s centralized control.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"20th CPC National Congress concludes in Beijing, Xi Jinping presides over closing session and delivers important speech","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-10-23","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/topnews/202210/23/content_WS63551debc6d0a757729e1950.html","claim":"The congress described Xi’s report as a political declaration and a program of action, and called on the party, military, and people to rally around Xi at the core of the Party Central Committee.","locator":"lines 45-62","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official congress record with explicit action-program language and formal adoption of the report and revised constitution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 congress period.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution because the resolution was adopted at the congress convened under Xi’s leadership and repeatedly centers Xi as the core.","contrary_evidence":["This is institutional self-description, not an independent audit of delivery."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.10"]}
- 2022-10-28 drafting process: Xi personally led the report drafting work throughout and chaired key meetings that shaped the final program.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Report to 20th CPC National Congress — How it was shaped","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-10-28","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/topnews/202210/28/content_WS63620a10c6d0a757729e22f0.html","claim":"Xi attached great importance to drafting the report, led the process throughout, served as head of the drafting team, and personally contributed new thoughts and measures.","locator":"lines 48-50 and 63-65","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official account of the drafting process with direct statements about Xi’s role in shaping the report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 reporting and drafting cycle.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution: the source directly assigns Xi ownership of the drafting process and content direction.","contrary_evidence":["Leadership over drafting does not prove successful execution of every listed objective."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6"]}
- 2022-06-02 implementation package: the State Council said it would speed up delivery of the stabilization package so market entities and households benefit from it.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China to expedite delivery of policy package to keep economy stable","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-06-02","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/premier/news/202206/02/content_WS6297eddec6d02e533532b9c5.html","claim":"China would speed up implementation of a package of policy measures to maintain stable economic performance, and the package was intended to improve the speed and effectiveness of policy implementation and ensure eligible firms and households benefited.","locator":"lines 45-52","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government implementation notice with concrete administrative instructions and timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate-to-strong attribution: the package was tied to the central policy orientation from the Central Economic Work Conference and Government Work Report, within Xi’s governing system.","contrary_evidence":["The notice describes implementation intentions; it does not by itself verify local compliance or results."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.6","8B.8"]}
- 2022-06-09 follow-through: State Council task forces inspected 12 provinces and reported bottlenecks, then ordered barrier removal and better policy effects.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China to remove bottlenecks in policy implementation to produce greater effects for keeping economic activity stable","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-06-09","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/premier/news/202206/09/content_WS62a1285ec6d02e533532be0c.html","claim":"After special inspections in 12 provinces, the State Council said it would remove barriers, improve support measures, and better bring out policy effects for steady growth, market entities, employment, foreign trade, and investment.","locator":"lines 45-50","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official record of inspection and corrective follow-up, not just announcement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate attribution through the State Council and central economic policy chain under Xi’s leadership.","contrary_evidence":["The existence of inspections implies implementation problems remained significant."],"lenses":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.8"]}
- 2022-04-15 monetary easing: PBC cut bank reserve requirements to support the real economy and market entities.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China cuts reserve requirements for banks as economy slows","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-04-15","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/china-cuts-reserve-requirements-for-banks-as-economy-slows-2804570","claim":"China cut reserve requirements for banks for the first time in 2022, releasing about 530 billion yuan in long-term liquidity to cushion a sharp slowdown and support firms affected by COVID-19 and weak property conditions.","locator":"Reuters article body, lines 422-432","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent reporting with concrete numbers and named policy action, though hosted on a syndication site.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 action and immediate context.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate attribution: the action is state policy under Xi’s governing system, but the source attributes the operational decision to the PBOC, not to Xi personally.","contrary_evidence":["Analysts quoted in the piece said the cut might be insufficient to reverse the slowdown."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.6","8B.7"]}
- 2022-11-13/14 property rescue: Reuters said regulators rolled out 16 steps to support property liquidity and ensure pre-sold home delivery.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Factbox-Key regulatory measures to stabilise China's embattled property sector","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-13","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/factboxkey-regulatory-measures-to-stabilise-chinas-embattled-property-sector-2942982","claim":"Chinese regulators outlined 16 steps to support the property sector, including financing for developers and special loans to ensure pre-sold home delivery, as part of a liquidity rescue.","locator":"Reuters factbox, lines 436-441","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent factbox with specific policy mechanics and direct references to regulator notices, though still second-hand.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate attribution: the measures came from PBOC and CBIRC within Xi’s policy system; direct personal direction is not shown in the article.","contrary_evidence":["The rescue package responds to a sector crisis that had been building since 2020, so it is partly corrective rather than wholly preventive."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.6","8B.8","8B.9"]}
- 2022-12-01 COVID adaptation after protests: Reuters reported an imminent easing of quarantine protocols and mass testing after unprecedented protests.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China set to loosen COVID curbs after week of historic protests","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/china-set-to-loosen-covid-curbs-after-week-of-historic-protests-2955191","claim":"China was set to ease quarantine protocols and reduce mass testing after anger over the toughest COVID curbs fuelled widespread protests, marking a major shift in policy.","locator":"Reuters article body, lines 420-429","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent reporting on a major policy reversal with specific contemporaneous timing and direct causal context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 period, late-year adaptation.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate attribution: the shift occurred under Xi’s regime after public pressure; the source does not prove his personal authorship but does show state adaptation at the top level.","contrary_evidence":["The shift appears reactive to public unrest, not a planned success from the outset."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.8","8B.9","8B.10"]}
- 2022 outcome check: final GDP verification put 2022 growth at 3.0 percent, far below the government’s 5.5 percent target.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Announcement of the National Bureau of statistics on the Final Verification of GDP in 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics of China","publication_date":"2023-12-29","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202401/t20240110_1946428.html","claim":"China’s final verified 2022 GDP growth was 3.0 percent at constant prices.","locator":"lines 23-25","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official statistical verification with exact annual GDP figure and revision status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures the 2022 outcome, albeit published later.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate attribution: the outcome reflects Xi-era macro policy, but also external shocks and inherited conditions.","contrary_evidence":["This is an aggregate outcome, not a pure measure of ruler competence; shocks and base effects mattered."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}
- 2022 household outcomes: real disposable income rose 2.9 percent overall, rural income grew faster than urban income, but consumption fell in real terms.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Households' Income and Consumption Expenditure in 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics of China","publication_date":"2023-01-18","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202301/t20230118_1892303.html","claim":"In 2022, nationwide real per-capita disposable income rose 2.9 percent, rural residents’ real disposable income rose 4.2 percent, and real per-capita consumption fell 0.2 percent.","locator":"lines 21-31","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official household-survey release with precise annual numbers and urban/rural breakdowns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 outcome data, published immediately after year-end.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate attribution: the numbers reflect Xi-era policy choices and constraints, but also pandemic and macro shocks.","contrary_evidence":["Consumption fell in real terms and urban consumption declined more sharply than rural consumption."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}
- 2022 innovation input: R&D spending rose 10.1 percent to 30,782.9 billion yuan, with intensity at 2.54 percent of GDP.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022年全国科技经费投入统计公报","publisher":"国家统计局 / 科学技术部 / 财政部","publication_date":"2023-09-18","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/sj/zxfb/202309/t20230918_1942920.html","claim":"China’s 2022 R&D spending reached 30,782.9 billion yuan, up 10.1 percent year on year, with R&D intensity at 2.54 percent of GDP and basic research up 11.4 percent.","locator":"lines 626-630","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official statistical communiqué with exact numeric series and category breakdowns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 input/outcome data, published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate attribution: strongly tied to Xi-era industrial and innovation priorities, though not uniquely attributable to one ruler decision.","contrary_evidence":["Higher R&D input does not itself prove efficient conversion into output or broad productivity gains within 2022."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}
- Independent external assessment: the World Bank said 2022 growth was expected to slow to 2.7 percent amid uneven recoveries after pandemic slowdowns.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China Economic Update - December 2022","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/video/2022/12/20/china-economic-update-december-2022","claim":"The World Bank said China’s economy had uneven growth in 2022 and expected real GDP growth to slow to 2.7 percent before a 2023 reopening rebound.","locator":"video transcript / description lines 48-49 and 189-189","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative external macro assessment with clear date and quantitative forecast, though it is a projection rather than realized year-end data.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 year and its end-state forecast.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate attribution: the Bank links the slowdown to pandemic cycles and policy support, but not to Xi personally alone.","contrary_evidence":["This is a forecast and summary assessment, not a post-hoc audit of implementation."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}
- Independent external assessment: the IMF said 2022 growth was below global growth for the first time in over 40 years because of pandemic restrictions, real-estate contraction, and weak consumption.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"People’s Republic of China: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; Staff Report; and Statement by the Executive Director for the People’s Republic of China","publisher":"IMF","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/002/2023/067/article-A001-en.xml","claim":"The IMF said China’s 2022 growth was expected to be below global growth for the first time in over 40 years, with pandemic restrictions, a deep real-estate contraction, and weak consumption as the main drags.","locator":"abstract / context section in the Article IV page","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"High-authority external assessment, but the accessible page is partly abstracted and the exact publication date is only available at issue level.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 period and its macro conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate attribution: the IMF attributes the downturn to a mix of policy, pandemic, property, and external factors rather than to Xi alone.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF statement is macro-level and does not isolate any single Xi decision as the sole cause."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- Considered roughly 30 documents across official CPC/gov.cn, PBC, NBS, Reuters syndications, Bloomberg graphics, World Bank, IMF, and other China-policy coverage.
- Retained a compact, non-duplicative set focused on explicit program articulation, drafting ownership, implementation packages, monetary easing, property rescue, zero-COVID reversal, and year-end outcome data.
- Rejected duplicate syndications of the same Reuters stories, broad explainers that added no new 2022 claim, and later 2023/2024 follow-ups when they only repeated the 2022 pattern without adding period-specific evidence.
- One IMF page opened poorly in the browser, so the search result snippet was used for the claim rather than relying on a partial open.
- I did not use purely contextual authoritarian/corruption material unless it also showed a direct nexus to program execution, coordination, or adaptation.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence of Xi personally managing day-to-day implementation is still limited; much of the execution record runs through the State Council, PBC, NBS, or party machinery.
- Provincial and subnational variation is under-evidenced here, especially on which localities executed the program well or poorly.
- The strongest negative evidence is outcome-based: growth and consumption missed the 2022 target, even though the state did mobilize policy responses.
- More independent evidence on private-sector confidence, productivity, and local compliance would improve attribution, but it is unlikely to change the broad picture of clear planning, real mobilization, mixed execution, and partial end-of-year progress.
